    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season died the way too many Maple Leafs seasons do - with promise on the lip and disappointment in the teeth. <strong>The Leafs finished 32-36-14, eighth in the Atlantic and 15th overall, a flat ending to a year that never found its spine.</strong> At 78 points and with an L7, the club spent too much of the schedule chasing the pack instead of setting the pace, and that 31-game gap to the division leader is the sort of number that tells the whole grim story. The work now shifts from denial to decision. Who stays, who goes, and who carries the blame - the coach, the front office, or the roster built to be better than this? The stars are no longer selling hope; they are selling accountability. The Leafs need a harder blue line, more reliable goaltending, and a roster that doesn't fold when the pressure tightens. The summer will bring contract talk, trade speculation, and the draft board, all of it under the same unforgiving light. <strong>What matters next is whether Toronto finally uses this collapse to reset the whole operation, not just shuffle the deck chairs.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 30, 2026"
      },
