    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "29-29",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "A club that was buried five runs deep yesterday came up swinging and came up alive - that's the kind of night that can change a room. <strong>Toronto's 6-5 comeback over Baltimore on Friday</strong> stretched the Blue Jays' surge to four straight and pushed them to 29-29, third in the AL East, still eight games back but suddenly looking less like a drifter and more like a team with a pulse. Vladimir Guerrero Jr. delivered the blow that mattered most, a two-run double in the eighth that turned a bad evening into a getaway map, and the Jays' latest escape fits the larger pattern: eight wins in their last 10, enough to suggest the bats are finally carrying their weight. Charles McAdoo also flashed in the frame, homering for his first career hit, the sort of small, bright detail that a dugout remembers. There's no room for complacency, though;.500 is still the line in the sand, and the pitching staff has to keep dragging itself through the day-to-day grind. <strong>Next up: Saturday afternoon at Baltimore, with the Blue Jays trying to turn a rescue act into a statement</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 30, 2026"
      },
